'''Lynne Marie Walz''' (née '''Shallberg'''; born June 18, 1963)<ref name=maidenname>{{cite news|title=Lynne Shallberg and Christopher Walz|newspaper=Fremont Tribune|date=July 16, 1986|page=7|access-date=July 14, 2026|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/524877869/}}</ref><ref name=middlename>{{cite news|title=Mainly About People|newspaper=Fremont Tribune|date=June 25, 1963|page=6|access-date=July 14, 2026|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/499572987/}}</ref> is an American politician, former teacher, and realtor, who served as a Nebraska state senator in the [[Nebraska Legislature]] from 2017 to 2025. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she represented District 15, which includes [[Fremont, Nebraska|Fremont]] and surrounding areas.

In November 2025, Walz formed an exploratory committee to explore running for governor in [[2026 Nebraska gubernatorial election|2026]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Olberding |first1=Matt |title=Former state senator exploring Democratic bid for Nebraska governor |url=https://nebraskapublicmedia.org/en/news/news-articles/former-state-senator-exploring-democratic-bid-for-nebraska-governor/ |access-date=November 24, 2025 |work=[[Nebraska Public Media]] |date=November 24, 2025}}</ref> Walz is currently the Democratic nominee for Governor after winning the primary in May 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Mueller |first1=Julia |title=Tim Walz’s distant relative Lynne Walz running for Nebraska governor |url=https://nebraskapublicmedia.org/en/news/news-articles/former-state-senator-exploring-democratic-bid-for-nebraska-governor/ |access-date=January 21, 2026 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=January 21, 2026}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Walz was born in [[Fremont, Nebraska]] and graduated from Arlington High School in 1981.<ref name="bio">{{Cite web|title=Biography « District 15 Blog|url=http://news.legislature.ne.gov/dist15/biography-2/|access-date=2021-04-26|website=news.legislature.ne.gov}}</ref> She then went on to [[Midland Lutheran College]] to earn a B.A. in elementary education in 1994. She married her husband in 1986 and now has 3 children and 4 grandchildren.<ref name=bio/> She is a distant cousin by marriage of Minnesota governor and [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 Democratic nominee for vice president]] [[Tim Walz]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mueller |first=Julia |date=January 21, 2026 |title=Tim Walz’s distant relative Lynne Walz running for Nebraska governor |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5699779-lynne-walz-nebraska-governor-campaign/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260130100846/https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5699779-lynne-walz-nebraska-governor-campaign/ |archive-date=2026-01-30 |url-status=live |access-date=2026-07-28 |work=The Hill}}</ref>

Walz is a former teacher and worked for [[Fremont, Nebraska#Education|Fremont Public Schools]] and [[Archbishop Bergan High School]]. She is a current realtor at Don Peterson and Associates.<ref name=bio/> Before entering the state legislature, Walz was on the Fremont Bergan School Board and served as the president of Fremont Board of Realtors.

== Political career ==

=== Election results ===
Walz was first elected to the state legislature in 2016, defeating incumbent [[David Schnoor]] with 51% of the vote. She was chosen as the running mate (Lieutenant Governor) of fellow State Senator [[Bob Krist]] in his [[2018 Nebraska gubernatorial election|2018]] campaign for Governor.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.omaha.com/news/politics/elections/state-sen-lynne-walz-chosen-as-democrat-bob-krist-s/article_227d8f0d-c285-5856-8a86-7b0fb668eaa8.html|title=State Sen. Lynne Walz chosen as Democrat Bob Krist's running mate|work=[[Omaha World-Herald]]|last=Gaardner |first=Nancy|date=April 30, 2018|access-date=May 2, 2018}}</ref> The Krist-Walz ticket lost the election receiving 41% of votes.<ref>{{cite web|title=Official Results: General Election – November 6, 2018|url=https://electionresults.sos.ne.gov/resultsSW.aspx?text=Race&type=SW&map=CTY|publisher=Nebraska Secretary of State|access-date=9 December 2018}}</ref> Walz won reelection on November 3, 2020, against David Rogers with 59.6% of the votes.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Nebraska Secretary of State - Election Night Results - November 3rd, 2020|url=https://electionresults.nebraska.gov/resultsSW.aspx?text=Race&type=LD&map=DIST|access-date=2021-03-29|website=electionresults.nebraska.gov}}</ref> Walz was term limited in 2025.

=== Committees ===
At the beginning of the 2017 Legislative Session, Walz sat on the Education and Natural Resources Committees. From 2019 until 2025, she sat on both the Health and Human Services and Education Committee.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lynne Walz|url=https://ballotpedia.org/Lynne_Walz|access-date=2021-03-29|website=Ballotpedia}}</ref>

In 2021, she ran for Education Chair and won against incumbent Senator [[Mike Groene]]. Chairs are elected through a secret ballot and the final vote was 25–23.<ref>{{Cite web|first1=Martha|last1=Stoddard|first2=Paul|last2=Hammel|title=Walz unseats Groene as chair of Education Committee in Nebraska Legislature|url=https://fremonttribune.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/walz-unseats-groene-as-chair-of-education-committee-in-nebraska-legislature/article_8343c0a4-1856-55f4-b202-7ab068966557.html|access-date=2021-03-29|website=Fremont Tribune}}</ref> The secret ballot initiative has led to several Senators attempting rule changes every year.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Walton|first=Don|title=Nebraska Legislature votes to keep secret balloting for committee chairs|url=https://omaha.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/nebraska-legislature-votes-to-keep-secret-balloting-for-committee-chairs/article_c4dc7c5e-59fe-5d8c-9521-5dbc884a3fe8.html|access-date=2021-03-29|website=Omaha.com}}</ref>

